es are internally consistent. This is a data inconsistency in how monetary charges are presented. (2) The risk level dual characterisation (6 blended vs 5 and 7 individual) is a presentation inconsistency but has limited bearing on the factual accuracy of client details — it is an internal label mismatch. (3) The most material inconsistency is the cashflow modeller input of £2,206 per month against stated current expenditure of approximately £3,710 per month. These figures are used in the same report to characterise the client's financial position, and the lower figure (~£2,206) appears to have been used as the modelling input without explanation of why it differs so significantly from the stated current outgoings. This is a material data inconsistency that affects a figure driving the cashflow-based recommendation. (4) The £60,000 vs £57,120 GIA discrepancy is plausibly explained by the £2,880 net pension contribution being deducted from the inheritance remainder, but this reconciliation is absent from the report — leaving an unexplained £2,880 gap between stated intention and recommended action. (5) The employment status / pension contribution tension is a genuine internal contradiction: the report cannot simultaneously assert the client has stopped working and that earnings are sufficient to support the contribution, especially while acknowledging it is unsure of the earnings figure. The non-earner limit (£3,600 gross / £2,880 net) coinciding exactly with the recommended contribution suggests the report may be relying on the non-earner allowance rather than actual earnings, but this is not stated — leaving a factual ambiguity about the contribution basis. The most severe finding is item (3): the cashflow modeller using £2,206 per month against stated current expenditure of ~£3,710 per month is a material internal inconsistency in a figure that drives the cashflow projection underpinning the recommendation. Item (5) is also significant as it concerns the factual b