ximately £225,018 [P7/P20], consistent with liquid assets only; the £669,092 figure presumably includes property and other non-liquid assets but this reconciliation is not demonstrated in the passages available.","The report does not explicitly reconcile that the intended GIA investment of 'remaining £60,000' of inheritance differs from the actual recommended GIA amount of £57,120 (shortfall £2,880), though plausibly explained by the net pension contribution amount — this is not stated in the report.","ONS life expectancy figures cited by the report (male 84, female 87) do not exactly match any retrieved passage; nearest values in passages are 85 and 88 [P13/P17], though these are chart label extractions and may not be directly comparable stated figures."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The comparison can be made. Several issues emerge:\n\n1. EXPENDITURE INCONSISTENCY (material): The most significant discrepancy is between the suitability report's own stated current expenditure (~£3,710/month combining essential, discretionary and holidays) and the figure used in the cashflow model (~£2,206/month or £29,915 p.a. including tax & NI). The Fact Find [P1] corroborates the £2,206.67 monthly figure as the 'Calculated Total Monthly Household Expenditure', and this is what was fed into the cashflow model. However, the suitability report separately describes higher expenditure in its current situation narrative. The canonical model flags this as an internal inconsistency. The cashflow modelled figure of £2,206/month appears to exclude discretionary and holiday spend that the report's own narrative includes. This means the no-shortfall conclusion rests on an expenditure assumption materially lower than the report's own described spending level. This is a data inconsistency of moderate severity — the cashflow inputs differ from the separately stated expenditure in the report.\n\n2. YVETTE'S INCOME FIGURE (moderate): The Fact Find records Yvette's net