{"groupId":"G7.5","requirement":"Benefit versus cost — fair value: where the recommendation increases cost, the suitability report must clearly explain the benefits expected in exchange for those costs, and those claims must be supported by and consistent with the evidence.","reportSays":"The report acknowledges the new pension arrangement costs more than the existing one: existing plan 0.54% / £427 per annum vs new plan 0.62% / £512.69 per annum, a difference of +0.08% / £63. The report states the proposed plan must outperform the current plan by £63 in year one and by approximately 1.08% annually (including adviser fee). It asserts this is achievable based on past performance of recommended funds and improved risk alignment. Benefits cited are: (1) lower platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21% via agency terms; (2) risk alignment — existing funds at risk level 6 are too high for the client's Cautious Balanced profile of 4, and the new fund is aligned to risk 4; (3) professional oversight via ongoing monitoring; (4) tax-efficient contribution of £3,600 gross with 20% tax relief; (5) platform integration across pension, ISA and GIA on a single Aviva platform. The cost justification states 'this small increase in charges is justified by the improved risk alignment and ongoing professional oversight.' Critical yield is stated as 2.94% mid-growth rate.","fileSays":"Research report [P8] confirms the new fund (Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II) is mapped to risk level 4, matching the client's agreed risk level. Fund factsheet [P12] confirms the total fund charge of 0.41% (AMC 0.30% + additional expenses 0.11%). Performance data in [P8] and [P11] confirms Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus Fund II 5-year cumulative return of 36.21% and 1-year return of 11.92% to 31 October 2025. The existing plan review report [P2/P9] shows the existing combined strategy had 5-year cumulative return of 58.01% (Vanguard LifeStrategy blend) and 1-year return of 15.91% to 31 October 2025. The pension switch research report [P7] records the existing funds' discrete performance. Cashflow models [P23/P24] show ISA and GIA fee inputs as '0.5% ongoing platform/fund charges and 1% ongoing financial planning fee' — yet the suitability report states platform charge of 0.21% plus fund charges, not 1.5% total. No provider illustration with a formal RIY comparison or cumulative cost impact over the investment horizon is present in the evidence pack. The illustration path /solution/0/illustrationProvided is flagged as NotPresentInDocument by the extraction. The 'achievability' of 1.08% required outperformance is supported only by the report's own assertion referencing past performance; however the 5-year performance of the recommended fund (36.21%) is materially lower than the existing combined strategy (58.01%) over the same period, and 1-year performance is also lower (11.92% vs 15.91%). The report does not address this comparison or explain how the recommended fund is expected to outperform the higher-performing existing fund net of the additional 1.08% annual cost drag.","discrepancies":["The report claims the required outperformance of 1.08% annually 'is achievable given the improved risk alignment and professional oversight,' but the evidence shows the recommended fund has delivered lower cumulative returns than the existing strategy over both 1-year (11.92% vs 15.91%) and 5-year (36.21% vs 58.01%) periods — the report does not reconcile this.","No provider illustration (RIY, projected values, cumulative cost impact) is present in the evidence pack — the extraction flags /solution/0/illustrationProvided as NotPresentInDocument — meaning the cumulative cost impact of the higher charges over the investment horizon to age 60 is never shown.","Cashflow models [P23] and [P24] input ongoing fees for the ISA and GIA as '0.5% ongoing platform/fund charges and 1% ongoing financial planning fee' (total 1.5%), whereas the suitability report's charges tables show platform 0.21% plus fund charges of 0.29% (ISA/GIA) = 0.50% total, with the ongoing adviser charge separately stated — the cashflow fee assumption is materially higher than the disclosed product/platform charges alone and the basis is not reconciled in the report.","The derived figures show the monetary charge on the new pension (£512.69 at 0.62%) implies a fund value base of ~£82,692 — which is the post-contribution value — while the existing plan charge (£427 at 0.54%) uses the pre-contribution value of ~£79,092. The report does not flag that the monetary comparison is on different bases, making the £63 difference potentially understated.","The benefit of 'lower platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21%' is partially offset by the new investment management charge of 0.41% vs existing 0.22%, resulting in a net increase — the report acknowledges this net increase but presents the platform reduction as a standalone benefit without clearly noting the fund charge increase more than offsets it in the summary benefits list."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report correctly identifies and discloses the cost increase (+0.08% / +£63 per annum on the pension switch) and provides a critical yield figure (2.94% / 1.08% required outperformance). However, the justification for achievability is weak in light of the evidence. The guard states that an assertion that required outperformance 'is achievable' is a claim, not an analysis, and requires supporting performance data before being accepted. Here, the supporting performance data in the file actually contradicts the claim: the recommended fund's 5-year return (36.21%, [P11]/[P12]) is materially lower than the existing combined strategy (58.01%, [P9]), and 1-year performance is also lower (11.92% vs 15.91%). The report's past-performance basis for achievability therefore runs counter to the evidence rather than being supported by it. The absence of a provider illustration (flagged as NotPresentInDocument) means there is no cumulative cost impact or RIY comparison over the horizon to age 60, which is a disclosure shortfall for a case with a higher-cost recommendation. The cashflow model fee inputs (1.5% total in [P23]) do not align with the suitability report's stated charges (0.50% product/platform), and this inconsistency is unexplained. The different monetary bases used in the switch charges table (pre-contribution for existing, post-contribution for new) are noted in the extraction ambiguities and the derived figures, and while the percentage difference is accurate, the presentation is potentially misleading. The vulnerability overlay does not materially affect this finding — no vulnerability characteristics are identified in the evidence that would heighten the concern beyond the general population standard. Overall, the achievability claim is unsupported by the performance evidence provided, and a key disclosure component (cumulative cost illustration) is absent.","citations":[{"passageId":"P8","source":"Personal Pension Plan research report 03-12-2025 11-35.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva Insured Funds Multi-asset Plus Fund II (GBP)","5.58","12.61","11.92","33.59","36.21","70.66"]},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Personal Pension Plan review report 03-12-2025 15-17.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Combined Performance (GBP)","6.89","16.25","15.91","42.07","58.01","136.32"]},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Aviva Insured Funds Multi-Asset Plus II S14 - Factsheet.md","category":"H","quote":"Figures quoted below are based on prices which contain an AMC of 0.30% and Additional Expenses of 0.11%, which gives a total fund charge of 0.41%.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P23","source":"Cashflow - Market Crash.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva ISA","Fees","0.5% ongoing platform/fund charges and 1% ongoing financial planning fee"]},{"passageId":"P23","source":"Cashflow - Market Crash.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva GIA","